@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@code{emacs-devtools-mcp} aims to do for Emacs what
@uref{https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp,
chrome-devtools-mcp} does for Chrome: give an LLM-driven coding agent a
structured set of tools for inspecting state, exercising input, and
verifying that changes actually rendered.

The server runs @emph{inside} the user's Emacs session as an Elisp package
and exposes Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools over a Unix-domain socket.
A small POSIX-sh relay (@file{bin/emacs-devtools-mcp}) bridges between the
agent's stdio MCP transport and the socket.  Tools that need a clean Emacs
to experiment in --- bisecting an @file{init.el}, running a startup
profile, or testing under @code{emacs -Q} --- can target a subordinate
Emacs daemon spawned via @code{spawn_emacs}.

This manual is the reference.  See @file{README.md} in the source tree for
the quickstart and a usage transcript.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@section Requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item GNU Emacs 30.1 or newer.
@item @command{socat} and @command{jq} on @env{PATH}.
@item For headless / GUI tests: @command{xvfb-run} and @command{grim}.
@end itemize

The repository ships a @file{shell.nix} that pins all of the above.

@section From source

@example
git clone https://github.com/cjprice/emacs-devtools-mcp.git
cd emacs-devtools-mcp
nix-shell --run 'make all'
@end example

@section Loading the server

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/emacs-devtools-mcp/lisp")
(require 'emacs-devtools-mcp)
(require 'emacs-devtools-mcp-server)
(emacs-devtools-mcp-server-start)
@end example

@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-server-start} binds a Unix socket at
@file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/edmcp/$NAME.sock}, where @code{$NAME} comes from
@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-server-name} (default @code{"default"}).  It hard
fails if @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} is unset.  @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-server-stop}
shuts the listener down and removes the socket and token sibling file.

@section Registering with Claude Code

Copy @file{.mcp.json.example} to a project's @file{.mcp.json} (or to your
global Claude Code config) and edit the @code{command} field to the
absolute path of @file{bin/emacs-devtools-mcp}:

@example
@{
  "mcpServers": @{
    "emacs-devtools": @{
      "command": "/abs/path/to/emacs-devtools-mcp/bin/emacs-devtools-mcp",
      "env": @{ "EDMCP_NAME": "default" @}
    @}
  @}
@}
@end example

The agent's first frame (the @code{initialize} request) is rewritten by the
relay to carry the per-launch token from
@file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/edmcp/$NAME.token}.

@node Architecture
@chapter Architecture

@example
agent --stdio--> bin/emacs-devtools-mcp --unix socket--> Host Emacs
   newline-delimited JSON-RPC                                |
   per the MCP stdio spec                                    |
                                                             v
                                       make-process argv list
                                                             |
                                                             v
                                       Subordinate Emacs (`emacs -Q --bg-daemon')
                                       (optionally under `xvfb-run')
@end example

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Transport}.  MCP stdio is newline-delimited JSON.  The package
subclasses the abstract @code{jsonrpc-connection} from @code{jsonrpc.el}
and supplies newline framing, keeping the dispatch / continuation
machinery.  (The built-in @code{jsonrpc-process-connection} uses
Content-Length framing and is therefore wrong for MCP.)

@item
@strong{Concurrency}.  Single in-flight slot per server.  Tools tagged
@code{:fast} or @code{:slow}; @code{:slow} runs inside @code{while-no-input}
+ @code{with-timeout} so user keystrokes preempt them.  A second
@code{:slow} request returns a @code{busy} envelope.

@item
@strong{Spawn target}.  Subordinate Emacs is launched with
@code{--bg-daemon=NAME}; @code{emacsclient -s NAME --eval} is the call
boundary.  Replies are parsed under @code{(let ((read-eval nil)) (read
@dots{})}) so a malicious @code{#.} reader-macro in subordinate output
cannot execute code in the host.
@end itemize

@node Conventions
@chapter Conventions

@section The @code{target} parameter

Every tool accepts an optional @code{target} object:

@example
@{ "host":  true @}              ;; default --- the user's Emacs
@{ "spawn": "<handle>" @}        ;; a subordinate started by `spawn_emacs'
@end example

@noindent
@code{target} is always an object.  Bare strings are not accepted.

@section Cursors

List-shaped tools (@code{list_buffers}, @code{list_faces},
@code{describe_keymap}, @code{describe_hooks}, @code{list_handles},
@code{list_messages}, @code{list_warnings}, @code{trace_log}) take an
optional @code{cursor} string and return @code{next_cursor} when more
results remain.  Pass that opaque string back as @code{cursor} to continue.
Cursor state is held server-side, keyed by random 16-byte hex token, with
a 5-minute TTL.

@section JSON keys

All wire keys are @code{snake_case}: @code{server_name},
@code{print_level}, @code{max_bytes}, @code{next_cursor}.  Internal Elisp
identifiers stay kebab-case; the registration macro maps between them.

@section Error envelopes

Two distinct failure shapes:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Protocol failures} --- unknown tool, schema-invalid arguments ---
return a JSON-RPC @code{error} object with code @code{-32601} (method not
found) or @code{-32602} (invalid params).
@item
@strong{Tool execution failures} --- @code{eval} signal, buffer not found,
allowlist violation, payload too large --- return a successful response
with @code{isError: true} plus an MCP content block describing the
failure.  The wire form for false in JSON is @code{:json-false}; agents
should test for truthiness explicitly.
@end itemize

@section Tool annotations

Every tool exposes MCP @code{readOnlyHint}, @code{destructiveHint}, and
@code{idempotentHint} on @code{tools/list}.  Agents that respect these
hints can plan accordingly: @code{init_lint} is read-only and idempotent,
while @code{bisect_init} is destructive (executes user code in
subprocesses) and not idempotent (the predicate may have side effects).

@node Server tools
@chapter Server tools

@deffn Tool ping &key message target
Smoke-test tool: replies with @code{"pong"}, optionally echoing
@code{message} verbatim.  Read-only and idempotent.

Example:

@example
@{ "name": "ping", "arguments": @{ "message": "hi" @} @}
=> @{ "pong": "hi" @}
@end example
@end deffn

@node Spawn tools
@chapter Spawn tools

The spawn subsystem manages subordinate @code{emacs -Q --bg-daemon}
processes.  Handles are random opaque strings; the daemon's
@code{server-name} is auto-generated as @code{edmcp-spawn-<HANDLE>} and is
not caller-controllable, which prevents an agent from colliding with a
daemon owned by the user.

@deffn Tool spawn_emacs &key init headless target
Spawn a subordinate Emacs daemon and return its handle.  @code{init} is
optional; when set it must lie under
@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-init-allowlist}.  @code{headless} is reserved for
Xvfb-backed mode and is currently rejected.  Returns
@code{@{handle, server_name, pid, expires_at@}}.  Destructive.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool attach_emacs &key server_name target
Register an externally started daemon by @code{server_name}.  Probes the
daemon before recording the handle; signals if no daemon answers.
@code{server_name} is validated against @code{[A-Za-z0-9_-]+}.  Destructive.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool list_handles &key cursor target
List active handles, paginated.  Each entry includes @code{idle_seconds}
and @code{expires_at} so an agent can plan around the idle reaper.
Read-only and idempotent.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool kill_emacs &key handle target
Kill the daemon at @code{handle} and forget the handle.  Destructive but
idempotent: killing an unknown handle returns OK rather than signalling.
@end deffn

@node Eval and debug tools
@chapter Eval and debug tools

@deffn Tool eval_elisp &key form print_level print_length target
Evaluate @code{form} in @code{target} and return its printed value, the
@samp{*Messages*} delta, and any error.  Output is redacted to drop lines
mentioning auth-source / epg / tramp before transit.  Destructive.

Example:

@example
@{ "name": "eval_elisp",
  "arguments": @{ "form": "(+ 1 2)" @} @}
=> @{ "value": "3", "messages": [], "error": null @}
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Tool edebug_instrument &key function target
@deffnx Tool edebug_uninstrument &key function target
Instrument @code{function} for edebug, or remove instrumentation.  The
original @code{symbol-function} value is recorded so re-uninstrument does
not require re-reading source.  Destructive but idempotent.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool capture_backtrace &key form target
Evaluate @code{form} and return any signaled backtrace.  A
@code{signal-hook-function} captures the live frame stack at signal time,
so the trace reflects the point of failure rather than the point where the
package's @code{condition-case} caught.  Output is tail-truncated at
@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-backtrace-max-bytes} and redacted.  Destructive.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool trace_function &key function buffer target
@deffnx Tool untrace_function &key function target
@deffnx Tool trace_log &key buffer cursor target
@code{trace-function-foreground} round-trip.  @code{trace_log} returns the
lines of the trace buffer (default @code{trace-buffer}), paginated and
redacted.  @code{trace_function}/@code{untrace_function} are destructive
and idempotent.  @code{trace_log} is read-only and idempotent.
@end deffn

@node Buffer tools
@chapter Buffer tools

@deffn Tool list_buffers &key filter cursor target
List live buffers, optionally narrowed by regex @code{filter}.  Read-only
and idempotent.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool buffer_state &key buffer target
Return point/mark/mode/file metadata for @code{buffer}.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool buffer_substring &key buffer start end with_properties max_bytes target
Return up to @code{max_bytes} of @code{buffer} between @code{start} and
@code{end}.  Sets @code{truncated: true} and @code{next_offset} when the
cap is hit, so an agent can stream the rest with follow-up calls.  When
@code{with_properties} is true, the returned text includes a
@code{properties} array describing text properties.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool list_messages &key n cursor target
Return the last @code{n} lines of @samp{*Messages*}, redacted.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool list_warnings &key cursor target
Return the contents of @samp{*Warnings*}, paragraph-split.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool ert_run &key selector target
Run ERT for @code{selector} (default: all tests) and return a summary
plist with pass/fail counts and per-test detail for failures.  Destructive.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool describe_hooks &key hook cursor target
List all bound hooks, or the contents of a specific @code{hook}.
@end deffn

@node Key tools
@chapter Key tools

@deffn Tool where_is &key command keymap target
Return all key sequences bound to @code{command} in @code{keymap}
(default: global).
@end deffn

@deffn Tool lookup_key &key keys keymap accept_default target
Return the command bound to @code{keys} in @code{keymap}.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool describe_keymap &key keymap_or_mode prefix cursor target
List flattened bindings under @code{keymap_or_mode}, optionally narrowed
by @code{prefix}.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool simulate_keys &key keys buffer target
Run @code{keys} as a @code{kbd} macro in @code{buffer} and return the
state delta: @code{last_command}, @code{this_command}, @code{point},
@code{mark}, @code{buffer_diff}, @code{messages}.  Text-cap-checked
against @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-keys-simulate-text-cap}.  Destructive.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool key_translation_trace &key keys target
Trace @code{keys} through @code{input-decode-map},
@code{local-function-key-map}, and @code{key-translation-map}, returning
the input/output of each stage.
@end deffn

@node GUI tools
@chapter GUI tools

@deffn Tool screenshot_frame &key frame format target
Export @code{frame} (default: selected frame) to a base64-encoded PNG and
return it as an MCP @code{image} content block (@code{@{type: "image",
data, mimeType@}}).  Returns a structured error when no display backend
is available.  The frame's pixel dimensions must fit within
@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-screenshot-max-pixels}; otherwise the image is
downscaled before encoding.

A backend probe at server-start picks between @code{x-export-frames},
@command{grim} (Wayland), and @command{xwd} (X11).  The result is cached
in @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-tools-gui--host-backend}.  The spawn target
always uses @code{x-export-frames} (the Xvfb daemon is X11).
@end deffn

@deffn Tool get_frame_tree &key frame target
Return frames @math{->} windows @math{->} buffer metadata for the
selected display.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool face_at &key buffer line column target
Return the face at @code{line} (1-based) and @code{column} (0-based) of
@code{buffer}.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool describe_face &key name target
Return inheritance-resolved attributes and docstring for face @code{name}.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool list_faces &key filter cursor target
List all face symbols in alphabetical order, optionally narrowed by
@code{filter} (regex).
@end deffn

@deffn Tool color_contrast &key foreground background target
Compute the WCAG contrast ratio between @code{foreground} and
@code{background}.  Pure math; identical results host vs.@: spawn.
@end deffn

@node Init tools
@chapter Init tools

@strong{Caveat.}  These tools execute the user's real @file{init.el} in
subordinate @code{emacs -Q --batch} subprocesses that inherit the user's
@env{HOME} and the rest of the environment.  Anything the init file does
on disk (creates files, mutates state, contacts the network) will happen
during a probe.  The host Emacs is unaffected because subordinates are
batch processes, but the user's filesystem is not.

@deffn Tool init_lint &key file target
Byte-compile @code{file} in @code{emacs -Q --batch} and report
diagnostics.  @code{file} defaults to @code{user-init-file} (or the XDG
path) and is allowlist-checked before execution.  The compiled output
goes to a temp directory and is discarded --- this tool does not modify
the user's config tree.  Read-only and idempotent.

Returns a plist with @code{file}, @code{exit_code}, @code{warnings},
@code{errors}, @code{ok}, and the redacted @code{raw} subprocess output.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool startup_profile &key file target
Run @code{file} under the CPU profiler in @code{emacs -Q --batch}.
Returns @code{elapsed} seconds plus the top 50 hotspot frames from
@code{profiler-cpu-log}, with frame strings passed through the redaction
layer.  Destructive (loads user code), not idempotent (profiler sampling
is wall-clock dependent).

Includes a @code{parse_error} flag set when the sentinel pair printed by
the wrapper isn't found in subprocess output --- this guards against an
@file{init.el} that prints fake sentinels of its own.
@end deffn

@deffn Tool bisect_init &key file predicate target
Binary-search @code{file} for the form that triggers @code{predicate}.
@code{predicate} is a Lisp form (string) that returns non-nil iff the
problem reproduces.  Each probe is one @code{emacs -Q --batch}
subprocess loading a prefix of @code{file}.  Returns the 1-based
@code{culprit_form} index plus its @code{line_start}/@code{line_end} and
a @code{load_failed_probes} counter that flags when convergence was
driven by load-time crashes rather than predicate hits.

Bisection assumes @code{predicate} is monotonic over prefixes of the
init file --- if the agent supplies a non-monotonic predicate (one that
toggles, depends on wall-clock, etc.), the result is undefined.  This is
why the tool advertises @code{idempotentHint: false}: even successful
runs may produce different culprits on repeated invocation.

The path is allowlist-checked.  Predicate errors are caught and treated
as @emph{non-reproducing} so that a buggy predicate cannot pin to an
arbitrary form.  The predicate string and the init file are both rejected
if they contain a reader macro (@code{#.} or @code{#@@}).
@end deffn

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

All knobs are @code{defcustom}s under one of four groups:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{emacs-devtools-mcp} --- parent.
@item @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-server} --- transport, dispatch, lifecycle.
@item @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-spawn} --- subordinate Emacs.
@item @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-tools} --- per-category tool defaults.
@item @code{emacs-devtools-mcp-security} --- auth, redaction, allowlist.
@end itemize

@noindent
@code{M-x customize-group RET emacs-devtools-mcp RET} walks the full set.

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-server-name
Socket name segment under @file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/edmcp/}.  Default
@code{"default"}.  Multiple Emacsen can each run a server with a different
name without colliding.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-init-allowlist
Directories from which @code{init_lint}, @code{startup_profile},
@code{bisect_init}, and @code{spawn_emacs} may load init files.  Default:
@file{~/.config/emacs}, @file{~/.emacs.d}, project root.  Paths are
expanded via @code{expand-file-name} + @code{file-truename} before
prefix-matching.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-max-response-bytes
Global hard cap on per-call response payload, in bytes.  Default
@code{262144}.  Exceeding it returns a @code{payload_too_large} tool-error
envelope.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-screenshot-max-pixels
Maximum pixel count for screenshot exports.  Default 1920@math{x}1080
(@code{2073600}).  Larger frames are downscaled before encoding.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-spawn-idle-timeout
Idle timeout, in seconds, for subordinate Emacs handles.  Default
@code{1800} (30 minutes).  The reaper runs on a timer and on
@code{kill-emacs-hook}.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-spawn-max-handles
Cap on the number of concurrently held subordinate handles.  Default
@code{4}.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-slow-tool-timeout
@code{with-timeout} ceiling for @code{:slow} tools, in seconds.  Default
@code{25}.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-bisect-max-probes
Hard cap on @code{bisect_init} iterations.  Default @code{12} --- enough
for an init.el of @math{2^@{12@}} = 4096 forms.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-init-batch-timeout
Per-probe timeout, in seconds, for @code{emacs -Q --batch} subprocesses
under @code{init_lint} / @code{startup_profile} / @code{bisect_init}.
Default @code{30}.
@end defopt

@defopt emacs-devtools-mcp-redact-extra-regexps
Additional regular expressions to scrub from @samp{*Messages*} /
backtrace / profiler-frame output before it leaves the host.  Default
@code{nil}.  The base set --- @code{auth-source-}, @code{epg-},
@code{tramp-} --- is always applied.
@end defopt

@noindent
Less-commonly-tuned knobs live alongside the relevant tool category and
are documented via @code{customize-group}: the @code{*-page-size}
variables for cursor pagination, @code{cursor-ttl-seconds},
@code{backtrace-max-bytes}, @code{trace-log-line-max-bytes},
@code{keys-simulate-text-cap}, @code{auth-log-failures},
@code{spawn-ready-timeout}, @code{spawn-emacs-program},
@code{spawn-emacsclient-program}.

@node Security
@chapter Security

This is a developer tool for the user's own Emacs session.  @code{eval_elisp}
is intentionally @strong{not sandboxed}, and @code{bisect_init} /
@code{startup_profile} execute arbitrary code from the user's real
@file{init.el} in subordinate @code{emacs -Q --batch} subprocesses.

@section What is defended

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Transport}.  Unix-domain socket at @file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/edmcp/},
mode @code{0600}.  Hard fails if @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} is unset; never
falls back to @file{/tmp}.  Stale socket files are verified
@code{S_ISSOCK} + same-uid + non-symlink before re-bind.
@item
@strong{Peer auth}.  @code{SO_PEERCRED} uid check on every accept, plus a
per-launch random token in the first MCP frame.  The token lives in a
sibling @code{0600} file at @file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/edmcp/$NAME.token};
the relay reads it and injects it into the @code{initialize} request's
@code{params._meta.token}.
@item
@strong{Subprocess hygiene}.  @code{make-process} with explicit argv
lists everywhere --- no @code{start-process-shell-command}.
@code{server_name} validated against @code{[A-Za-z0-9_-]+}.
@item
@strong{Reader-macro injection}.  Subordinate output is parsed under
@code{(let ((read-eval nil)) (read @dots{}))}.  Predicate strings and init
files are additionally rejected up-front if they contain @code{#.} or
@code{#@@} sequences.
@item
@strong{Init paths}.  Allowlist-checked via @code{expand-file-name} +
@code{file-truename} + prefix match against
@code{emacs-devtools-mcp-init-allowlist}.
@item
@strong{Output redaction}.  A regex layer scrubs lines mentioning
@code{auth-source-}, @code{epg-}, and @code{tramp-} (plus
@code{redact-extra-regexps}) from any @samp{*Messages*} / backtrace /
profiler-frame surface before it leaves the host.
@item
@strong{Resource caps}.  @code{with-timeout} on every @code{:slow}
handler; @code{max-response-bytes}; @code{screenshot-max-pixels};
@code{unwind-protect} around @code{profiler-start}.
@end itemize

@section What is not defended

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{eval_elisp} / @code{simulate_keys} / @code{capture_backtrace}
itself.  General-purpose execution by design.
@item Multi-tenant access.  Single-user only.
@item Networked transport.  Local stdio + Unix socket only.
@item Side effects from subordinate batch processes.  They run with the
user's @env{HOME}; an init file that writes files or contacts the network
will do so during a probe.
@end itemize

